     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose looks like an error.\n\n**Problem:** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to **5,100 mg/day**. That is double the FDA maximum for immediate-release metformin, which is 2,550 mg/day. The risks at that dose include lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity. Kidney and liver function are normal, so that doesn't change the limit.\n\n**Possible intended orders:**\n- **850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum.\n- **1,700 mg/day total.** For example, 850 mg PO twice daily with meals.\n- **500 mg PO twice daily or 1,000 mg PO twice daily.** These are common regimens, and starting low and titrating up lowers the risk of GI side effects.\n\nPlease confirm the dose and frequency you want, ideally with your resident or attending. If the patient is new to metformin, the usual start is 500 mg once or twice daily with meals, titrated up over weeks. Once you confirm, I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
